= A New President, An Uncertain Future

**28 July 1985 — Lima**

Alan García assumes the presidency of Peru following APRA's electoral victory.

The new government promises economic reform and greater national independence. It must also confront political violence and determine how civilian institutions will oversee the security forces.

Three priorities compete for the president's attention. Which direction will define the opening of the administration?

- @apra_debt_ceiling: Enforce the foreign-debt payment ceiling.
- @apra_peace_commission: Establish a civilian peace initiative.
- @apra_cabinet_consultation: Consult APRA's leadership.

@apra_debt_ceiling
title: Enforce the Foreign-Debt Payment Ceiling
on-arrival: {!
Q.support = Math.min(100, Q.support + 4);
Q.business_confidence = Math.max(0, Q.business_confidence - 5);
Q.govt_card_available = 0;
!}

You make economic independence the defining commitment of the administration. The decision energises some supporters but risks worsening relations with creditors.

These numerical effects represent the game's hypothetical consequences.

- @main: Return to the presidential desk

@apra_peace_commission
title: Establish a Civilian Peace Initiative
on-arrival: {!
Q.legitimacy = Math.min(100, Q.legitimacy + 4);
Q.military_confidence = Math.max(0, Q.military_confidence - 3);
Q.govt_card_available = 0;
!}

You prioritise civilian oversight and dialogue in responding to Peru's political violence. This may strengthen institutional credibility while creating friction with security officials.

- @main: Return to the presidential desk

@apra_cabinet_consultation
title: Consult APRA's Leadership
on-arrival: {!
Q.party_unity = Math.min(100, Q.party_unity + 5);
Q.support = Math.min(100, Q.support + 1);
Q.govt_card_available = 0;
!}

You consult party leaders before committing the government to its full programme. Cooperation improves, although disagreements over economic and security policy remain.

